{
  "pdf_page": 569,
  "book_page": 539,
  "verified_by": "claude-opus-5.5",
  "verified_on": "2026-10-16",
  "entries": [
    {
      "entry": 0,
      "root": "Zarâfah",
      "verdict": "plausible",
      "modern_form": "Arabic زَرَافَة zarāfa / زُرَافَة zurāfa 'giraffe' (ultimate origin disputed)",
      "note": "The Arabic word, both vocalizations and the gloss are right, and it is undisputed that the European words come from it. The ultimate source is the open question. Nourai's box note ('of an African root') follows Klein and Lokotsch, who suggest a Central-African origin. Current Wiktionary instead derives Arabic zurāfa from Classical Syriac zārīp̄ā / zarnāp̄ā, borrowed from Classical Persian zurnāpā 'flute-leg' (zurnā + pā). On that view the Persian zarrāfe would be a Persian > Syriac > Arabic > Persian round-trip. Devic and Pihan only record the Arabic form, and Pihan's folk derivation from zarafa 'to walk quickly' is not accepted. The root is therefore 'plausible': right as a word, uncertain as an origin.",
      "note_fa": "«زرافه» در عربی به دو صورت زَرافة و زُرافة به معنای همین جانور آمده است و همهٔ صورت‌های اروپایی از آن گرفته شده‌اند؛ تا اینجا سخن نورایی درست است. اما ریشهٔ دورتر واژه روشن نیست: کلاین و لوکوچ آن را از زبانی آفریقایی می‌دانند (همان یادداشت نورایی)، و ویکی‌واژه آن را از سریانی zārīp̄ā / zarnāp̄ā و آن را وام از فارسی «زُرناپا» (زرنا + پا، «نی‌پا») می‌گیرد. اگر این نظر درست باشد، «زرافه» در فارسی واژه‌ای است که از فارسی به سریانی و عربی رفته و دوباره به فارسی برگشته است. پس ریشه با احتیاط پذیرفتنی است.",
      "sources": [
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D8%B2%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%81%D8%A9",
        "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/giraffe"
      ],
      "ref_check": [
        {"ref": "KLN:657", "status": "partial", "note": "KLN_1966.txt leaf 355 (grep '^giraffe,'): 'giraffe, n. — F. girafe, It. giraffa, fr. Arab. zarāfah, which is prob. of African origin. The circumstance that Arab. z has become g (pronounced j) proves that the word came into the European languages through the medium of the Italians.' This supports the Arabic root and the Italian route. The leaf number suggests a printed page in the 680s rather than 657, so the page number may refer to another printing."},
        {"ref": "AHD:558", "status": "not_checked", "note": "AHD:558 is a main-dictionary page of the 1975 AHD (not the IE-roots appendix), and that edition is not available locally or online. The Watkins 1985 appendix has no 'giraffe' entry (grep -i giraffe AHD_watkins1985.txt: no hit)."}
      ],
      "consulted": [
        {"src": "LKT", "where": "no. 2234, pp. 173-174", "stance": "supports", "note": "'2234. Ar. zurāfa: \"Giraffe, Camelopardalis giraffa\" [sicherlich selbst ein Fremdwort und wie das Tier aus Zentralafrika stammend]; hieraus it. giraffa, frz. girafe, kat. sp. pg. girafa ... engl. ndl. giraffe' (grep 'Giraffe' LKT.txt)"},
        {"src": "DEV", "where": "s.v. Girafe (DEV.txt l.1420)", "stance": "supports", "note": "'Girafe. Esp. girafa (ancienn. azorafa), portug. girafa, ital. giraffa ... C'est l'arabe zourâfa, zerâfa. Meninski donne aussi ... sournapâ, zournapâ' (Devic also reports the Persian zurnāpā form)"},
        {"src": "PHN", "where": "s.v. GIRAFE (PHN.txt l.5338)", "stance": "partial", "note": "'[zerrâfet], dérivé de [zeref] marcher avec rapidité' (Arabic origin, internal Arabic folk derivation)"},
        {"src": "LAM", "where": "s.v. Girafe (LAM.txt l.4384)", "stance": "supports", "note": "'Girafe. Esp: girafa, jirafa (ancienn. azorafa). It: giraffa; de zarâfa, zourâfa'"},
        {"src": "SKT", "where": "s.v. Giraffe (SKT.txt l.7601)", "stance": "partial", "note": "'Giraffe ... (F.–Span.–Arab.–Egypt.) ... Arab. zarāf, zarāfa. From Egypt. soraphi, i.e. long neck (Mahn)' (Arabic agreed; Egyptian origin is an obsolete guess)"}
      ],
      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "lang": "Persian",
          "words": "zarrâfeh",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "Persian zarrāfe / zarāfe 'giraffe' is the Arabic zarāfa / zurāfa, borrowed with the Arabic tā' marbūṭa rendered as Persian -e (ـه). Mo'in's footnote in Borhan says the same ('از عربی زرافه'). The doubled -rr- in Nourai's transliteration matches the common Persian pronunciation zarrāfe. If the Syriac/Persian zurnāpā theory is right, this is a re-borrowing of an originally Persian coinage.",
          "derivation_fa": "«زرافه» در فارسی وام‌واژه‌ای از عربی زَرافة / زُرافة است و تای گرد عربی در فارسی به «ـه» بدل شده است. معین در حاشیهٔ برهان قاطع نیز می‌نویسد «از عربی زرافه». بنا بر نظر ویکی‌واژه، خود واژهٔ عربی از فارسی «زرناپا» از راه سریانی گرفته شده است. وام‌گیری فارسی از عربی قطعی است و ادعای نورایی درست است.",
          "sources": ["https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/%D8%B2%D8%B1%D8%A7%D9%81%D9%87"],
          "ref_check": [
            {"ref": "BQT:1009", "status": "supports", "note": "BQT_v2_pages.txt leaf 471 (≈ p.1014; grep 'زرافه'): headword '* زرافه - بفتح اول و چهارم ...' with Mo'in's footnote 'از عربی زرافه ... رک: دزی ج ۱ ص ۷۸۵ ... و ازین زبان وارد ف[رانسه] و انگلیسی giraffe و آلمانی Giraffe شده؛ نوعی از پستانداران ... افریقا'."}
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {"src": "MON", "where": "vajehyab (not fetched for this word)", "stance": "silent", "note": "Not looked up separately. Mo'in's own Borhan footnote, quoted in ref_check, already gives his view."},
            {"src": "ARY", "where": "ARY_pages.txt", "stance": "silent", "note": "grep 'زرافه': no hit (not an IE word)"},
            {"src": "LKT", "where": "no. 2234", "stance": "supports", "note": "Ar. zurāfa as the source of the European words; Persian not mentioned"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "lang": "Italian",
          "words": "giraffa",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "Italian giraffa (attested from the 13th-14th c.) is borrowed from Arabic zarāfa / zurāfa, probably through trade with Egypt and North Africa. Arabic z was rendered by Italian g(i) [dʒ], and Klein takes this as proof that Italian carried the word into the other European languages; Old Spanish azorafa kept the z. Nourai's note on the 'z' to 'j, g' change is correct.",
          "derivation_fa": "واژهٔ ایتالیایی giraffa وام از عربی زَرافة است. «ز» عربی در ایتالیایی به صدای «ج» (نوشتهٔ gi) درآمده است، و کلاین همین دگرگونی آوایی را نشانهٔ آن می‌داند که واژه از راه ایتالیایی به دیگر زبان‌های اروپایی رسیده است (اسپانیایی کهن azorafa هنوز «ز» را نگه داشته بود). ادعای نورایی و یادداشت او دربارهٔ ابدال z به j/g درست است.",
          "sources": ["https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/giraffa"],
          "ref_check": [
            {"ref": "KLN:657", "status": "supports", "note": "KLN_1966 leaf 355: 'It. giraffa, fr. Arab. zarāfah ... Arab. z has become g (pronounced j) proves that the word came into the European languages through the medium of the Italians.'"},
            {"ref": "AHD:558", "status": "not_checked", "note": "1975 AHD main-dictionary page not accessible"}
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {"src": "LKT", "where": "no. 2234", "stance": "supports", "note": "'hieraus it. giraffa, frz. girafe'"},
            {"src": "DEV", "where": "s.v. Girafe", "stance": "supports", "note": "'ital. giraffa ... C'est l'arabe zourâfa, zerâfa'"},
            {"src": "LAM", "where": "s.v. Girafe", "stance": "supports", "note": "'It: giraffa; de zarâfa, zourâfa'"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "lang": "French",
          "words": "girafe",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "French girafe (Middle French giraffe, 14th c.) comes from Italian giraffa. Old French had the earlier forms orafle (Joinville) and gerofle (Marco Polo, ed. Pauthier), taken more directly from Arabic (Devic), but they were replaced by the Italian-based form.",
          "derivation_fa": "واژهٔ فرانسوی girafe (فرانسوی میانهٔ giraffe) از ایتالیایی giraffa گرفته شده است. در فرانسوی کهن صورت‌های orafle و gerofle هم بوده که از عربی آمده بودند، اما صورت ایتالیایی جای آن‌ها را گرفت. ادعای نورایی درست است.",
          "sources": ["https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/giraffa", "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/giraffe"],
          "ref_check": [
            {"ref": "KLN:657", "status": "supports", "note": "KLN_1966 leaf 355: 'giraffe, n. — F. girafe, It. giraffa, fr. Arab. zarāfah'"}
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {"src": "DEV", "where": "s.v. Girafe", "stance": "supports", "note": "'On trouve en vieux français orafle (Joinville), giraffe; Marco Polo ... écrit gerofle'"},
            {"src": "LKT", "where": "no. 2234", "stance": "supports", "note": "'frz. girafe'"},
            {"src": "PHN", "where": "s.v. GIRAFE", "stance": "partial", "note": "gives the French word directly from Arabic [zerrâfet], with no Italian step"}
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "lang": "English",
          "words": "giraffe",
          "verdict": "confirmed",
          "derivation": "English giraffe (late 16th c.; the common name only from the 18th c., replacing camelopard) comes from French girafe / Middle French giraffe, which comes from Italian giraffa and ultimately from Arabic zarāfa.",
          "derivation_fa": "واژهٔ انگلیسی giraffe از فرانسوی girafe / giraffe و آن از ایتالیایی giraffa و در نهایت از عربی زَرافة است. این واژه در انگلیسی جای نام کهن‌تر camelopard را گرفت. ادعای نورایی درست است.",
          "sources": ["https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/giraffe"],
          "ref_check": [
            {"ref": "KLN:657", "status": "supports", "note": "KLN_1966 leaf 355: 'giraffe, n. — F. girafe, It. giraffa, fr. Arab. zarāfah'"},
            {"ref": "AHD:558", "status": "not_checked", "note": "1975 AHD main-dictionary page not accessible"}
          ],
          "consulted": [
            {"src": "SKT", "where": "s.v. Giraffe (l.7601)", "stance": "partial", "note": "'Giraffe ... (F.–Span.–Arab.–Egypt.) F. girafe – Span. girafa – Arab. zarāf, zarāfa' (routes via Spanish instead of Italian)"},
            {"src": "FSD", "where": "vol. 4 pronunciation list (FSD_vol4.txt l.63616)", "stance": "silent", "note": "'giraffe, ji-raf'' pronunciation only; no etymology found in the OCR"},
            {"src": "LKT", "where": "no. 2234", "stance": "supports", "note": "'engl. ndl. giraffe'"}
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "entry": 1,
      "root": "Zauša",
      "verdict": "plausible",
      "modern_form": "Iranian *zauša- 'raising a noise' (Morgenstierne's reconstruction from Pashto zwaž; not attested in Avestan)",
      "note": "Nourai's only reference, Gershevitch (AHM p.220, commentary to Yt 10.71), does not give *zauša- as an Avestan word. It is Morgenstierne's reconstruction (EVP) behind Pashto zwaž 'noise, clamour', and Gershevitch only suggests linking it to the Avestan epithet zušəm. So the label 'Avestan' is imprecise: the form is starred Proto-Iranian. Linking NP zōš 'violent, bad-tempered' with it is a possibility discussed on the same page, not an established etymology. Horn 676 offered Av. zōišnu-, and Hübschmann 676 rejected that ('sicher falsch'). Hübschmann noted that np. zōš can only go back to an Avestan-type *zaoš-, which fits *zauša- phonologically.",
      "note_fa": "ستاک *zauša- «بانگ برآوردن» واژه‌ای ثبت‌شده در اوستا نیست. این صورت بازسازی مورگنستیرنه برای پشتو zwaž «بانگ، غوغا» است، و گرشویچ (سرود مهر، ص ۲۲۰) فقط پیشنهاد می‌کند که آن را با صفت اوستایی zušəm پیوند دهیم. پس برچسب «اوستایی» دقیق نیست و باید «ایرانی باستانِ بازسازی‌شده» گفت. پیوند «زوش» فارسی با این ستاک از نظر آوایی ممکن است (هوبشمان هم گفته زوش فقط می‌تواند از *zaoš- آمده باشد)، اما قطعی نیست. پس با احتیاط پذیرفتنی است.",
      "sources": ["https://vajehyab.com/moein/%D8%B2%D9%88%D8%B4"],
      "ref_check": [
        {"ref": "AHM:220", "status": "partial", "note": "AHM.txt leaf 237 (= printed p.220+; grep 'raising a noise'): 'for the epithet av. zušəm ... a connection with Pš. zwaž \"noise, clamour\" may be suggested, which Morg., loc. cit., derives from *zauša-, \"raising a noise\"'. On the same page (OCR garbled) Morgenstierne's explanation of an NPers. word 'vehement, violent, wicked' as from '*a-zauša-' (EVP 106) is cited. The gloss is Nourai's source, but the form is reconstructed, not Avestan."}
      ],
      "consulted": [
        {"src": "HUB", "where": "no. 676, p.71 (leaf 81)", "stance": "partial", "note": "'676. Die Zusammenstellung ist sicher falsch, da zd. zōišnu- ... im Neup. nur *zēš ... lauten, umgekehrt np. zōš nur auf ein zd. *zaošnu- zurückgehen könnte' (rejects the Avestan comparison; implies a *zaoš- base)"},
        {"src": "HRN", "where": "no. 676, p.150 (leaf 171)", "stance": "partial", "note": "'676. zōš \"zornig, grob\". aw. zōišnu- \"unrein\" (nach Justi); arm. LW. (?) zōš \"hässlich\". Die Zusammenstellung ist sehr problematisch, wahrscheinlich sogar falsch'"}
      ],
      "nodes": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "lang": "Persian",
          "words": "ZÔŠ",
          "verdict": "plausible",
          "derivation": "Early New Persian zōš (Mo'in: archaic 'quick-tempered, harsh-natured, angry; strong'), attested in Rudaki's verse quoted by Nourai and by Asadi's Loghat-e Fors. Old Iranian -auš- regularly becomes NP -ōš (cf. gauša- > gōš 'ear'), so zōš can descend from *zauša-. The semantic chain 'raising a noise' > 'shouting, violent, bad-tempered' is reasonable, but no Middle Persian intermediate is attested. The derivation rests on Morgenstierne's Pashto comparison and is not certain. Horn's older comparison with Av. zōišnu- was rightly rejected by Hübschmann.",
